---
#1651: a `Matches` pattern with polynomial ambiguity (`\d*\d*` splits a run of
digits between its two loops in quadratically many ways) is an error unless a
length predicate bounds the input. Here `MaxLength` does, so it is a warning.
The `MaxLength` is written *after* the `Matches`: the emitter checks length
predicates first wherever they are written, so the bound caps the regex either
way.
---
commons demo.codes

type Code = String where Matches("\\d*-?\\d*") && MaxLength(16)

fn accepts(s: String) -> Bool {
  match Code.of(s) {
    Ok(_) => true
    Err(_) => false
  }
}

fn admits(s: String) -> Bool {
  s is Code
}
